# Security policy

## Reporting a vulnerability

Do not open a public issue for a suspected vulnerability.

**Email [{{email}}](mailto:{{email}}).**

GitHub private vulnerability reporting is the better channel, because it
attaches the report to the repository with a draft advisory and a CVE request
path already in place. It is a public-repository feature and it is **off by
default**, so switch it on before listing it here:

```bash
gh api -X PUT repos/{{packageName}}/private-vulnerability-reporting
```

Once the repository's `/security/policy` page shows a *Report a vulnerability*
button, name that channel above email. Until it does, the channel does not
exist, and advertising it sends a reporter to a button that is not there.

## Scope

This package ships icon assets and the provider that registers them with
`ichava/core`.

Core owns sanitisation, caching, seeding and the HTTP surface, so a defect in
how an icon is sanitised, cached or served belongs to
[ichava/core](https://github.com/ichava/core) rather than here. What belongs
here is the vendored assets themselves, this package's own registration code,
and anything in its build or sync tooling that widens what a refresh can reach.
